{
  "boardId": "brd_24280cb0c7614d36",
  "tenantId": "tnt_vibes",
  "name": "Recurring Maintenance",
  "stages": [
    {
      "key": "due",
      "name": "Due",
      "order": 0,
      "ownerKind": "human"
    },
    {
      "key": "running",
      "name": "Running",
      "order": 1,
      "ownerKind": "capability",
      "owner": "maintenance",
      "wipLimit": 2,
      "instructions": "Do the maintenance task this card names, on this host, and leave a record a person can audit later.\n\n  where      ./.maint/<cardId>/ inside the directory you start in, so two cards never collide.\n  scope      exactly what the card's title and spec ask for. A maintenance card is not an\n             invitation to tidy anything else you notice — if you find something else wrong,\n             say so in your handoff and let a human queue it.\n\nRead the card's spec before you start. If it names a command, run that command rather than one you\njudge equivalent: the point of a recurring card is that the same thing happens the same way every\ntime, and a substitution is invisible once it is in the past.\n\nReport what you actually did, not what the card asked for — those differ exactly when something\ninteresting happened, and that difference is the only reason anyone reads a maintenance log.\n\nComplete with a `summary` in your handoff: what ran, what changed, and anything you chose not to\ntouch. One honest paragraph beats a checklist of ticks.\n\nIf the task cannot be done — a host is unreachable, a credential has expired, the thing it\nmaintains no longer exists — **block the card with the reason** rather than completing it with a\nsummary that says you did nothing. A recurring card that quietly completes having done nothing is\nworse than one that stops, because it will quietly complete every week.",
      "completion": {
        "handoff": [
          "summary"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "verify",
      "name": "Verify",
      "order": 2,
      "ownerKind": "capability",
      "owner": "security",
      "instructions": "Check that the maintenance this card records actually happened, and say so independently.\n\nYou are not re-reading the previous stage's handoff and agreeing with it. You are looking at the\nsystem it claims to have changed. If the handoff says a backup ran, look for the backup. If it says\na certificate was renewed, read the certificate's expiry.\n\n  evidence   prefer something you observed over something you were told. A command's output you\n             ran yourself is evidence; a sentence in the handoff is a claim.\n\nComplete with a `verdict` in your handoff: `confirmed` plus what you observed, or `unconfirmed`\nplus what you looked for and could not find.\n\n**`unconfirmed` is a legitimate and useful answer.** A verify stage that always confirms is a\nrubber stamp, and this board has one job that a rubber stamp destroys: telling a person whether\nthe maintenance is real. If you could not check something, say which thing and why — do not\naverage it into a pass.",
      "completion": {
        "handoff": [
          "verdict"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "sign-off",
      "name": "Sign-off",
      "order": 3,
      "ownerKind": "human",
      "gate": "approval"
    },
    {
      "key": "closed",
      "name": "Closed",
      "order": 4,
      "ownerKind": "human"
    }
  ],
